// Arm64GtRuntime — the hand-assembled GREEN-THREAD backend code for AArch64, the arm64 twin of
// `Targets/X64/X64GtRuntime`. Everything else in the scheduler is builder-built `StdModule`
// (`Runtime/GtRuntime.maxon`, `Runtime/SchedRuntime.maxon`); these six pieces cannot be, because each
// names SP, a register PAIR fixed by an ABI list, or a frame no prologue built:
//
//   • `__gt_context_switch` — moves SP between two green threads' stacks and saves the physical
//     callee-saved file by hand.
//   • `__gt_trampoline` — entered by a `ret` into a frame nothing established.
//   • `__gt_morestack` — runs on the M's system stack, which it switches to itself.
//   • `__gt_stack_relocate` — moves a stopped green thread's stack, for a grow or a shrink, and REWRITES the
//     saved-x29 chain of the copy.
//   • `mrt_darwin_libc_call` — the door every libSystem call takes onto the M's system stack.
//   • the prologue STACK GUARD — emitted INLINE into ordinary compiled functions, below the `TargetOp`
//     tier, exactly as its x64 twin is.
//
// ⭐⭐ **WHAT THIS FILE TOOK FROM x64 AND WHERE IT DEPARTS.** The ALGORITHMS are x64's, ported step for
// step — the same saved-register contract, the same relocate-and-walk morestack, the same THREE-null gate
// in front of the current-GT read (the TLS slot, the M, and the M's current GT — see `THE THREE NULLS`
// below). Four things differ, and every one of them is an ISA fact rather than a preference:
//
//   1. **NO NT_TIB, ANYWHERE.** Half of x64's context switch and a third of its shim exist to keep
//      `gs:[0x08]`/`gs:[0x10]` describing the stack that is actually executing, because a Win32 kernel
//      call PROBES those two words and faults when RSP is outside them. Darwin has no such contract, so
//      `GtOffTibStackBase`/`GtOffTibStackLimit` are never read or written on this lane and `__gt_spawn`
//      does not seed them (see `GtRuntime.buildGtSpawn`'s per-target arm).
//   2. **THE RETURN ADDRESS IS A REGISTER, NOT A STACK WORD.** x64's switch ends in `ret`, which POPS
//      the resumed thread's continuation; AArch64's `ret` branches to x30. So a fresh green thread's
//      seed frame must place `&__gt_trampoline` in the x29/x30 PAIR the switch pops last, which is why
//      the frame is laid from the TOP DOWN in pre-indexed pairs and the LR lands at `frame_base + 8`
//      rather than at `stackTop - 8`. That is a STRUCTURAL difference in `__gt_spawn`, not a number.
//   3. **THE GUARD MUST PRESERVE x30 ITSELF.** x64's guard `call`s `__gt_morestack` and the guarded
//      function's own return address is already on the stack; here it is live in x30 and the prologue
//      has not run. The cold path therefore opens a 16-byte cell for it — and that cell is why the
//      projection below adds `Arm64GuardSaveBytes`.
//   4. **THE DOOR COPIES TWO STACK WORDS, NOT AN ARGUMENT AREA.** x64's shim moves a Win64 call's 5th+
//      arguments onto the system stack. AAPCS64 passes eight in registers, so the only stack argument a
//      libSystem call here takes is a VARIADIC one, and the door carries the caller's one 16-byte
//      outgoing region across with the switch.
//
// ⭐ **THE CONTEXT SWITCH TAKES TWO ARGUMENTS AND TOUCHES NO `.data` AT ALL.** The Std-tier strand runner owns
// the current-GT store (`GtRuntime.emitGtExecute`) — the same property x64's switch has, and the
// reason a fresh green thread can read its own GT out of x1 (the switch leaves `to` untouched, exactly
// as x64 leaves it in rdx).
//
// CALLING CONVENTION — AAPCS64 as `StdToArm64Conversion` uses it: integer arguments in x0..x7, primary
// return in x0, the throwing-call error flag in x9 (`Arm64FlagReturnReg`); callee-saved are x19..x28 and
// d8..d15 (`Arm64PrologueEpilogue`'s exported bounds) plus x29/x30. The context switch saves EXACTLY that
// set, because a `bl __gt_context_switch` is an ordinary call from a suspending function's view — and,
// unlike any ordinary callee, it runs ANOTHER green thread's arbitrary code before control comes back.

// ==========================================================================
// The saved-register block, DERIVED from the prologue's own callee-saved bounds
// ==========================================================================

// ⭐⭐ **THE ONE FACT THE Std TIER AND THIS ONE SHARE: a fresh green thread's initial frame is every
// callee-saved register plus the frame record.** `__gt_spawn` lays `gtInitialFrameBytes(target)` bytes and
// this chunk pops exactly that many; a mismatch is not a compile error but a `ret` into a wild address on
// the very first switch-in. So the size is DERIVED here from the four bounds the prologue pass exports and
// CHECKED against the Std constant (feedback: DERIVE > CHECK > COMMENT), which is what makes adding a
// callee-saved register a panic on the next async compile rather than a corrupted stack.
//
// ⚠ The registers move in PAIRS (`stp`/`ldp`), so each half of the callee-saved file must have an EVEN
// count — asserted rather than assumed, because an odd one would silently drop the last register.
function arm64GtSavedPairCount() returns RegCount
	let gprCount = Arm64CalleeSavedGprEnd - Arm64CalleeSavedGprLow
	let fpCount = Arm64CalleeSavedFpEnd - Arm64CalleeSavedFpFirst

	if gprCount mod Arm64GtRegistersPerPair != 0 or fpCount mod Arm64GtRegistersPerPair != 0 'oddFile'
		panic("arm64GtSavedPairCount: the context switch moves callee-saved registers in `stp`/`ldp` PAIRS, but the callee-saved GPR range holds {gprCount} registers and the SIMD range {fpCount} — an odd count would leave the last register of that file unsaved across every green-thread switch, which is a silent wrong answer rather than a crash")
	end 'oddFile'

	// The list the switch actually emits, so this size and those instructions cannot come apart.
	return arm64GtSavedPairs().count()
end 'arm64GtSavedPairCount'

// Two registers to an `stp`. Named because `arm64GtSavedPairCount` divides by it and a bare 2 in that
// expression says nothing about which of the several 2s in this file it is.
let Arm64GtRegistersPerPair = 2

// ⭐ Enforce the cross-tier byte-match. Called at chunk-build time, so an async compile for arm64 panics
// the moment the two sides disagree.
function assertArm64GtFrameConsistent(target Target)
	let framedBytes = (arm64GtSavedPairCount() as ParsedInt) * Arm64GtPairBytes
	let declared = gtInitialFrameBytes(target)

	if framedBytes != declared 'drift'
		panic("arm64 GT frame drift: the context switch saves {arm64GtSavedPairCount()} register pairs = {framedBytes} bytes, but `gtInitialFrameBytes` (the frame `__gt_spawn` lays for this target) is {declared} — a mismatch is a wild `ret` on the first switch-in, because the trampoline address `__gt_spawn` writes would not be the word the last `ldp` puts into x30")
	end 'drift'
end 'assertArm64GtFrameConsistent'

// The switch's two arguments. `to` rides through UNMODIFIED so a fresh green thread's trampoline reads its
// own GT out of x1 — the AArch64 spelling of x64's "`to` stays in rdx".
let Arm64GtSwitchFromReg = Arm64Register.x0
let Arm64GtSwitchToReg = Arm64Register.x1

// The switch's one scratch: it shuttles SP through a GPR, because SP is not a load/store SOURCE or
// DESTINATION on AArch64 (only a base), so `str sp, [x0]` does not exist.
//
// ⚠ **x10 AND NOT x9.** x9 is `Arm64FlagReturnReg` — the register a throwing call's error flag comes back
// in — and while no `tryCall` reaches this chunk today, a scratch chosen one register away from a
// live-across-calls ABI role is a trap for the next reader rather than a saving.
let Arm64GtSwitchSpScratch = Arm64Register.x10

// The trampoline's own registers. The GT must survive the user call, so it takes a CALLEE-SAVED register —
// safe to clobber here because the switch has just restored x19..x28 from this fresh stack's ZEROED seed
// frame, so they belong to nobody. The argument buffer and the function pointer are caller-saved
// NON-ARGUMENT registers (x10..x15), so the six argument loads below cannot clobber either.
//
// ⚠ **x10/x11 RATHER THAN x16.** x16 is this backend's import-call trampoline scratch
// (`arm64EmitImportCall`'s `adrp/ldr/blr x16`) and an AAPCS64 intra-procedure-call temporary a linker
// veneer may rewrite at any `bl`; holding a live function pointer there is sound only for as long as
// nothing inserts a veneer. A caller-saved general register has no such asterisk.
let Arm64GtTrampGtReg = Arm64Register.x19
let Arm64GtTrampFuncPtrReg = Arm64Register.x10
let Arm64GtTrampArgBufReg = Arm64Register.x11

// ==========================================================================
// `__gt_context_switch(x0 = from, x1 = to)`
// ==========================================================================

// Save the current green thread's callee-saved register + SP/FP state into `from`, restore `to`'s, and
// `ret` — which on AArch64 branches to the x30 the last `ldp` restored: a resumed thread's await-loop
// continuation, or, for a fresh one, `__gt_trampoline`. No frame, no `.data`, no OS call.
//
// ⚠ **`gt.fp` IS WRITTEN AND NOT READ BACK, AND THAT IS THE ISA DIFFERENCE AGAIN.** x64 restores rbp from
// `to->fp` because rbp is NOT one of its pushed registers; here x29 rides the frame-record pair, so the
// destination stack's own copy is the authoritative one and loading the field would be a second opinion
// about it. The field is still maintained, because `__gt_morestack` relocates it and a stale pointer into
// a freed stack is exactly the kind of thing a later reader would trust.
function buildArm64GtContextSwitchChunk(target Target) returns FunctionCodeChunk
	assertArm64GtFrameConsistent(target)
	var asm = Arm64RuntimeAsm.beginRawFunction()

	emitArm64GtSavedBlock(asm, direction: Arm64GtBlockDirection.push)

	// from->sp = SP ; from->fp = x29.
	arm64EmitMovFromSp(asm.code, dest: arm64RuntimeReg(Arm64GtSwitchSpScratch))
	arm64EmitStoreWord64At(asm, src: Arm64GtSwitchSpScratch, base: Arm64GtSwitchFromReg, byteOffset: GtOffSp)
	arm64EmitStoreWord64At(asm, src: Arm64Register.x29, base: Arm64GtSwitchFromReg, byteOffset: GtOffFp)

	// SP = to->sp — from here on we are on the destination green thread's stack.
	arm64EmitLoadWord64At(asm, dest: Arm64GtSwitchSpScratch, base: Arm64GtSwitchToReg, byteOffset: GtOffSp)
	arm64EmitMovToSp(asm.code, src: arm64RuntimeReg(Arm64GtSwitchSpScratch))

	emitArm64GtSavedBlock(asm, direction: Arm64GtBlockDirection.pop)
	arm64EmitRet(asm.code)

	return asm.finishChunk(GtContextSwitchName)
end 'buildArm64GtContextSwitchChunk'

// Which way the saved block moves. A union of two cases rather than a bool, so the emitter below cannot be
// called with an argument whose meaning has to be remembered.
enum Arm64GtBlockDirection
	push
	pop
end 'Arm64GtBlockDirection'

// ⭐⭐ **THE SAVED BLOCK, EMITTED FROM ONE LIST IN BOTH DIRECTIONS.** Push order is
// `x19/x20 … x27/x28 · d8/d9 … d14/d15 · x29/x30`; pop order is that list read BACKWARDS, which the
// pre-indexed `stp` / post-indexed `ldp` pair then makes balance by construction. Writing the two sequences
// out separately is how they would come to disagree — and a disagreement here is not a crash at the switch,
// it is a register file that silently belongs to the wrong green thread.
//
// ⚠ **THE FRAME RECORD IS LAST IN THE LIST, WHICH PUTS IT AT THE LOWEST ADDRESS** — at `gt.sp` itself, with
// x29 at `gt.sp + 0` and x30 at `gt.sp + 8`. `GtRuntime.emitGtSeedInitialFrame`'s arm64 arm writes
// `&__gt_trampoline` at exactly that second word. Reverse this order and a fresh green thread returns into
// whatever the seed zeros happen to be.
function emitArm64GtSavedBlock(asm Arm64RuntimeAsm, direction Arm64GtBlockDirection)
	let pairs = arm64GtSavedPairs()

	for i in 0 upto pairs.count() 'eachPair'
		let index = match direction 'order'
			push gives i
			pop gives pairs.count() - 1 - i
		end 'order'

		let pair = try pairs.get(index) otherwise panic("emitArm64GtSavedBlock: index derived from count is in range")
		emitArm64GtSavedPair(asm, pair: pair, direction: direction)
	end 'eachPair'
end 'emitArm64GtSavedBlock'

// One pair moved. The four spellings — GPR/SIMD × push/pop — are chosen HERE and nowhere else, so a file
// whose callee-saved lists grow a register never has to be edited in two places.
function emitArm64GtSavedPair(asm Arm64RuntimeAsm, pair Arm64GtSavedPair, direction Arm64GtBlockDirection)
	match pair.file 'file'
		gpr then emitArm64GtSavedGprPair(asm, first: pair.first, direction: direction)
		simd then emitArm64GtSavedFpPair(asm, first: pair.first, direction: direction)
	end 'file'
end 'emitArm64GtSavedPair'

function emitArm64GtSavedGprPair(asm Arm64RuntimeAsm, first MachineWord, direction Arm64GtBlockDirection)
	match direction 'dir'
		push then arm64EmitPushPairGpr(asm.code, rt1: first, rt2: first + 1)
		pop then arm64EmitPopPairGpr(asm.code, rt1: first, rt2: first + 1)
	end 'dir'
end 'emitArm64GtSavedGprPair'

function emitArm64GtSavedFpPair(asm Arm64RuntimeAsm, first MachineWord, direction Arm64GtBlockDirection)
	match direction 'dir'
		push then arm64EmitPushPairFp(asm.code, rt1: first, rt2: first + 1)
		pop then arm64EmitPopPairFp(asm.code, rt1: first, rt2: first + 1)
	end 'dir'
end 'emitArm64GtSavedFpPair'

// Which register FILE a saved pair belongs to — the one thing that decides between `stp Xt` and `stp Dt`,
// and which the raw encoding field cannot say (x19's GPR field and d19's SIMD field are both 19).
enum Arm64GtRegisterFile
	gpr
	simd
end 'Arm64GtRegisterFile'

// One `stp`/`ldp`'s worth of the saved block: which file, and the encoding field of its FIRST register (the
// second is always the next one up).
type Arm64GtSavedPair
	export var file as Arm64GtRegisterFile
	export var first as MachineWord

	export static function create(file Arm64GtRegisterFile, first MachineWord) returns Arm64GtSavedPair
		return Self{file: file, first: first}
	end 'create'
end 'Arm64GtSavedPair'

typealias Arm64GtSavedPairArray = Array with Arm64GtSavedPair

// ⭐ The block's contents, DERIVED from the prologue pass's own callee-saved bounds rather than restated.
// Add a callee-saved register to either file there and it is saved across a green-thread switch here, with
// `assertArm64GtFrameConsistent` catching the initial frame that did not grow with it.
//
// The encoding FIELD is the register's own number in both files (x19's GPR field is 19; d8's SIMD field is
// 8, `Arm64FirstVectorRegister` having already been subtracted out of the bounds the prologue pass states).
function arm64GtSavedPairs() returns Arm64GtSavedPairArray
	var pairs = Arm64GtSavedPairArray.create()
	arm64GtAppendPairs(pairs, file: Arm64GtRegisterFile.gpr, first: Arm64CalleeSavedGprLow, limit: Arm64CalleeSavedGprEnd)
	arm64GtAppendPairs(pairs, file: Arm64GtRegisterFile.simd, first: Arm64CalleeSavedFpFirst, limit: Arm64CalleeSavedFpEnd)

	// The x29/x30 frame record, LAST — see `emitArm64GtSavedBlock` for why that position is load-bearing.
	pairs.push(Arm64GtSavedPair.create(Arm64GtRegisterFile.gpr, first: arm64RuntimeReg(Arm64Register.x29)))
	return pairs
end 'arm64GtSavedPairs'

function arm64GtAppendPairs(pairs Arm64GtSavedPairArray, file Arm64GtRegisterFile, first RegNum, limit RegNum)
	var reg = first

	while reg < limit 'pairs'
		pairs.push(Arm64GtSavedPair.create(file, first: reg as MachineWord))
		reg = reg + Arm64GtRegistersPerPair
	end 'pairs'
end 'arm64GtAppendPairs'

// ==========================================================================
// `__gt_trampoline`
// ==========================================================================

// The entry a FRESH green thread `ret`s into on its first switch-in. NOT a normal call: no incoming
// register arguments, no prologue, and x29 is the seed frame's zero — which is what stops the panic
// backtrace at a green thread's boundary instead of walking into the scheduler context that switched it in.
//
// It reads its own GT out of x1 (the switch left `to` there), loads the async function and its argument
// buffer from the struct, fills every `MaxAsyncArgs` argument register, calls, captures x0 into
// `gt.result` and x9 into `gt.threw`, marks the GT completed, and switches to its waiter. It never
// returns; the `brk #0` is the trap for the impossible fall-through.
//
// ⚠ **x9 IS THE ERROR FLAG, NOT x1.** `Arm64FlagReturnReg` is x9 (`StdToArm64Conversion.lowerTryCall`
// reads it), and a trampoline that captured x1 would store the callee's SECOND ARGUMENT REGISTER as
// "did it throw" — a wrong answer on every `try await`, with nothing to crash on.
//
// ⚠ **NO SHADOW SPACE AND NO SP RESERVATION.** AAPCS64 obliges a caller to reserve nothing at all; SP is
// `stackTop` here (page-aligned, so 16-aligned) and the callee opens its own frame.
function buildArm64GtTrampolineChunk() returns FunctionCodeChunk
	var asm = Arm64RuntimeAsm.beginRawFunction()

	arm64EmitMovRuntimeReg(asm, dest: Arm64GtTrampGtReg, src: Arm64GtSwitchToReg)
	arm64EmitLoadWord64At(asm, dest: Arm64GtTrampArgBufReg, base: Arm64GtTrampGtReg, byteOffset: GtOffArgBuf)
	arm64EmitLoadWord64At(asm, dest: Arm64GtTrampFuncPtrReg, base: Arm64GtTrampGtReg, byteOffset: GtOffFuncPtr)

	// Every argument slot into its ABI argument register. The callee reads only the slots it declared; the
	// rest are the argument buffer's slab zeros in unread registers, which is why there is no arity to
	// carry. `arm64ArgRegisterAt` is the ABI's own oracle, so this order cannot drift from a call site's.
	for k in 0 upto MaxAsyncArgs 'args'
		arm64EmitLoadWord64At(asm, dest: arm64ArgRegisterAt(k as ParamCount), base: Arm64GtTrampArgBufReg, byteOffset: k * GtArgSlotBytes)
	end 'args'

	arm64EmitBranchLinkReg(asm.code, reg: arm64RuntimeReg(Arm64GtTrampFuncPtrReg))
	arm64EmitStoreWord64At(asm, src: Arm64IntReturnReg, base: Arm64GtTrampGtReg, byteOffset: GtOffResult)
	arm64EmitStoreWord64At(asm, src: Arm64FlagReturnReg, base: Arm64GtTrampGtReg, byteOffset: GtOffThrew)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64GtTrampFuncPtrReg, value: GtStatusCompleted)

	// ⛔⛔ **`STLR`, NOT `STR` — THIS IS THE PUBLICATION, AND THE TWO STORES ABOVE ARE WHAT IT PUBLISHES
	// (G3).** A reader on ANOTHER M — an awaiter's registration, `__gt_is_complete` — tests
	// `gt.status == completed` and then reads `gt.result` / `gt.threw`. On this weakly-ordered ISA a plain `str`
	// here lets the flag become visible before the two words it announces, and the awaiter reads a result the
	// thread had not written yet — **a wrong awaited
	// value, not a crash**, which no golden on this lane could see. The store-release orders both of them
	// before itself. Its acquire partner is `GtRuntime.emitGtLoadPublishedStatus`, which every reader of this
	// word goes through; neither half is a fix alone.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT WAS LATENT UNTIL THE Ms BECAME REAL AND IT IS NOT ANY MORE.** MAC3 made `osThreadCreate` a real
	// `pthread_create` here (and L4 a real `clone` on arm64-linux), and a `spawn` program measures six to
	// seven concurrently ACTIVE worker Ms on this host. `StdOp.atomicCas`'s note tracked exactly this shape
	// — "a store-load pair with no CAS between them" — as the one to watch for.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE ARG-BUFFER REGISTER IS THE SCRATCH**: `STLR` has no displacement field, so the field address
	// must be materialised, and that register's last read was the argument-marshalling loop far above.
	arm64EmitStoreReleaseWord64At(asm, src: Arm64GtTrampFuncPtrReg, base: Arm64GtTrampGtReg, byteOffset: GtOffStatus, scratch: Arm64GtTrampArgBufReg)

	// __gt_context_switch(from = gt, to = gt.waiter) — never returns; the strand runner that switched this thread
	// in set its waiter first.
	arm64EmitLoadWord64At(asm, dest: Arm64GtSwitchToReg, base: Arm64GtTrampGtReg, byteOffset: GtOffWaiter)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeReg(asm, dest: Arm64GtSwitchFromReg, src: Arm64GtTrampGtReg)
	arm64EmitBranchLink(asm.code, callFixups: asm.callFixups, targetName: GtContextSwitchName)
	arm64EmitBrk(asm.code)

	return asm.finishChunk(GtTrampolineName)
end 'buildArm64GtTrampolineChunk'

// ==========================================================================
// The current-M / current-GT read — the hand-assembled twin of `SchedRuntime.emitLoadCurrentM`
// ==========================================================================

// ⭐⭐ **`gtDest` = THE GREEN THREAD THIS OS THREAD IS RUNNING, BRANCHING TO `bail` IF THERE ISN'T ONE.**
// Every hand-assembled reader — the prologue stack guard, the system-stack door and the backtrace walker
// (`Arm64PanicRuntime.emitArm64WalkStackBounds`) — needs it, and it is `M->currentGt`. Writing the sequence
// twice is exactly how two of them would come to disagree about which of its nulls to test; the x64 file
// states the same argument, and the two lanes must not drift on which struct the field lives in.
//
// **THE THREE NULLS, AND ALL THREE MUST BE ASKED:**
//   * a ZERO SLOT OFFSET means `__sched_init_procs` has not run. On Windows the unchecked read would hand
//     back `NT_TIB.ExceptionList`; here it would read `tsd[0]`, a real pthread slot holding a real pointer
//     to something that is not an M. Reachable: the prologue guard runs inside `main` before the first
//     `async`.
//   * a ZERO M means this OS thread is not one — reachable by any thread that runs real code without
//     becoming one.
//   * a ZERO `currentGt` means the M is between green threads.
//
// ⚠ **THE SLOT READ IS A LOAD AND NOT A CALL, WHICH IS WHY THIS FITS IN A GUARD AT ALL.** Darwin's TSD
// array is at the masked thread pointer and `pthread_getspecific(key)` is `tsd[key]`; measured on this host,
// `tsd[key] == (TPIDRRO_EL0 & ~7) + key*8`. `StdToArm64Conversion.lowerTlsSlotLoad` carries the measurement
// and the assertion that keeps it honest — this is the same read, one tier down.
module function emitArm64LoadCurrentGtOrBail(asm Arm64RuntimeAsm, mDest Arm64Register, gtDest Arm64Register, scratch Arm64Register, bail BlockId, os Os)
	emitArm64LoadCurrentMOrBail(asm, dest: mDest, scratch: scratch, bail: bail, os: os)
	arm64EmitLoadWord64At(asm, dest: gtDest, base: mDest, byteOffset: MOffCurrentGt)
	arm64EmitCbzTo(asm, reg: gtDest, label: bail)
end 'emitArm64LoadCurrentGtOrBail'

// Branch to `bail` unless `addr` lies inside `gt`'s own stack, `[stackBase, stackBase + stackSize)`; a
// `stackBase` of 0 — an M's scheduler context, on a real OS-thread stack — bails too. Past both bails `scratch`
// holds the stack's top and `gt` its size. UNSIGNED compares: these are addresses. The twin of
// `X64GtRuntime.emitGtBailUnlessOnItsOwnStack`.
module function emitArm64GtBailUnlessOnItsOwnStack(asm Arm64RuntimeAsm, gt Arm64Register, scratch Arm64Register, addr Arm64Register, bail BlockId)
	arm64EmitLoadWord64At(asm, dest: scratch, base: gt, byteOffset: GtOffStackBase)
	arm64EmitCbzTo(asm, reg: scratch, label: bail)
	arm64EmitCmpRuntimeReg(asm, lhs: addr, rhs: scratch)
	arm64EmitCondBranchTo(asm, cond: Arm64CondCode.lo, label: bail)
	arm64EmitLoadWord64At(asm, dest: gt, base: gt, byteOffset: GtOffStackSize)
	arm64EmitAddRuntimeReg(asm, dest: scratch, lhs: scratch, rhs: gt)
	arm64EmitCmpRuntimeReg(asm, lhs: addr, rhs: scratch)
	arm64EmitCondBranchTo(asm, cond: Arm64CondCode.hs, label: bail)
end 'emitArm64GtBailUnlessOnItsOwnStack'

// ⭐⭐ **A FOURTH NULL, AND IT EXISTS ON ONE LANE ONLY.** On Darwin the thread pointer is written by the
// kernel for every thread that has ever existed, so it cannot be zero and no test is emitted — which is why
// this is not folded into the sequence above unconditionally: an extra `cbz` in the PROLOGUE GUARD is paid by
// every guarded function on the hot path, and it would move every arm64-macOS golden for a test that can
// never fire.
//
// On Linux `TPIDR_EL0` is what `mrt_host_tls_alloc` made it, and it is ZERO on any OS thread that never asked
// the scheduler for a slot. The offset test above cannot cover that: the offset is a `.data` word shared by
// the whole process, so it is non-zero the moment ONE thread has initialised the scheduler, while a second
// thread's own pointer is still 0. Without this the indexed load below is `[0 + key*8]` — a wild load a page
// into the null page rather than a diagnosable answer.
//
// ⚠ **THIS TEST IS WHY SUCH A THREAD IS SAFE, NOT A CLAIM THAT IT CANNOT EXIST.** A worker M gets its block
// through `CLONE_SETTLS` before it runs an instruction, so it never takes this bail — but a thread that reached
// Maxon code without one would, and one instruction turns that from a wild load into a diagnosable answer.
function arm64LinuxEmitBailOnNullThreadPointer(asm Arm64RuntimeAsm, reg Arm64Register, bail BlockId, os Os)
	match os 'os'
		macos then break 'os'
		linux then arm64EmitCbzTo(asm, reg: reg, label: bail)
		windows then panic("arm64LinuxEmitBailOnNullThreadPointer: there is no arm64-windows backend — `buildBackend` dispatches arm64 to this backend only for macos/linux")
		wasi then panic("arm64LinuxEmitBailOnNullThreadPointer: wasm32-wasi is not an arm64 lane — a WASI component has no `.text` this fragment could be spliced into")
	end 'os'
end 'arm64LinuxEmitBailOnNullThreadPointer'

// The M alone, with both of ITS nulls tested. Split from the function above because the door needs the M
// AFTER it has the GT (it reads the M's system stack out of it), so the two answers cannot share one
// register — and because the guard's cold path wants the M and nothing else.
function emitArm64LoadCurrentMOrBail(asm Arm64RuntimeAsm, dest Arm64Register, scratch Arm64Register, bail BlockId, os Os)
	arm64EmitAdrpAddPair(asm.code, rdataRelocs: asm.rdataRelocs, dest: arm64RuntimeReg(scratch), label: SchedTlsTebOffsetLabel, adrpKind: RdataRelocKind.dataSectionArm64Adrp, pageOffKind: RdataRelocKind.dataSectionArm64PageOff)
	arm64EmitLoadWord64At(asm, dest: scratch, base: scratch, byteOffset: 0)
	arm64EmitCbzTo(asm, reg: scratch, label: bail)
	arm64EmitThreadPointer(asm.code, dest: arm64RuntimeReg(dest), source: arm64ThreadPointerSource(os))
	arm64LinuxEmitBailOnNullThreadPointer(asm, reg: dest, bail: bail, os: os)
	arm64EmitLoadRegOffset(asm.code, dest: arm64RuntimeReg(dest), base: arm64RuntimeReg(dest), index: arm64RuntimeReg(scratch))
	arm64EmitCbzTo(asm, reg: dest, label: bail)
end 'emitArm64LoadCurrentMOrBail'

// ==========================================================================
// `__gt_morestack` — the stack grower — and `__gt_stack_relocate`, which it shares with a shrink
// ==========================================================================
//
// ⭐ THE HIGHEST-RISK PIECE IN THE LADDER: a chain-walk bug is a silent stack-corruption use-after-free.
// The SOUNDNESS argument is `X64GtRuntime`'s and is not re-derived here. In one line: no frame address is live
// where a stack moves, so the only stack→stack pointers are the saved-x29 chain links and `gt.sp`/`gt.fp`, and
// return addresses point into `.text`, which does not move. Relocation is therefore copy + rebase the live x29 +
// walk the chain adding the offset to each interior link. Frame pointers on EVERY function
// (`Arm64PrologueEpilogue`'s first paragraph, leaves included) is what pays for that.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE ONE ISA DIFFERENCE IN THE RELOCATION: A PARKED THREAD's x29 IS ALSO ON ITS STACK.** x64's switch keeps
// rbp only in `gt.fp`. This lane's switch pushes the x29/x30 record LAST, so `[gt.sp]` holds the x29 the
// switch-in restores, beside the copy in `gt.fp`; `__gt_stack_relocate` rebases that word as well as `gt.fp`,
// then walks the chain from `gt.fp`. At a grow the same word is the guard's saved x30 — a `.text` address, which
// the old-stack range test leaves alone — so the one routine is right in both directions without being told
// which it is serving.
//
// ENTRY CONTRACT (from the prologue guard): x11 = the current GT, x12 = this M's system-stack top, x14 = the
// guard's PROJECTED SP, SP = the GT stack pointing at the guard's saved-x30 cell, x30 = the return into the
// guard. Everything it saves goes on the SYSTEM stack, so the near-overflow GT stack never holds this frame.
//
// ⭐⭐ **IT GROWS UNTIL THE FRAME THAT ASKED FITS, AND REFUSES PAST `GtMaxStackBytes` OR A REFUSED `mmap`** —
// x64's `buildGtMorestackChunk` carries why, and the arithmetic is the same: the new stack must hold everything
// above the projected SP plus the guard height below it.
//
// ⭐ WHAT IT PRESERVES, AND WHY THAT SET. The guard runs at offset 0 of the entry block, BEFORE the prologue
// and before the `param` captures, so the guarded function has established nothing: every incoming argument
// is still in x0..x7 / d0..d7 and every callee-saved register still holds its CALLER's value. What must be
// saved is (live at a function entry) ∩ (destroyed by this chunk or its callees):
//   • x0..x7 — the integer arguments, which are also `mmap`'s and `__gt_stack_relocate`'s argument registers.
//   • x19..x28 — this chunk's own working registers, holding the caller's values.
//   • x29 — the chain link, which this chunk REBASES in its saved slot so the restore below hands back the
//     relocated pointer (x64 does exactly this with rbp).
//   • x30 — the return into the guard. AAPCS64 obliges nothing here: the `bl mmap` destroys it.
//   • d0..d7 — the float arguments. This chunk emits no SIMD instruction at all, but `mmap`/`munmap` are
//     ordinary AAPCS64 callees and d0..d7 are call-clobbered, so they come back destroyed. d8..d15 need no
//     save for the mirror-image reason: the ABI obliges libSystem to preserve them.

let Arm64MorestackEntryGtReg = Arm64Register.x11
let Arm64MorestackEntrySysTopReg = Arm64Register.x12

// The projected SP rides a caller-saved non-argument register, so nothing is live in it at a function entry and
// the register block need not save it. It is consumed by the growth sizing before anything else writes it.
let Arm64MorestackEntryProjectedSpReg = Arm64Register.x14

// The chunk's persistent body registers — all callee-saved, so `mmap` and `__gt_stack_relocate` preserve them,
// and all inside the block saved above.
let Arm64MoreGtReg = Arm64Register.x19
let Arm64MoreOldBaseReg = Arm64Register.x20
let Arm64MoreOldSizeReg = Arm64Register.x21
let Arm64MoreNewSizeReg = Arm64Register.x22
let Arm64MoreNewBaseReg = Arm64Register.x23

// The caller-saved temporaries the growth sizing and the frame arithmetic run in. None is live across a call in
// this chunk, and none is an argument register at the point it is used. `Arm64MoreSpReg` carries the ferried SP
// in and the relocated SP out, across the register restores, which do not touch it.
let Arm64MoreTempA = Arm64Register.x9
let Arm64MoreTempB = Arm64Register.x10
let Arm64MoreSpReg = Arm64Register.x13

// The system-stack frame. Slot 0 is the ferried GT stack pointer; the saved register block follows it. The
// two are separated so the ferried word can be read without addressing off a register the restores overwrite.
let Arm64MorestackEntrySpSlot = 0 as MachineWord
let Arm64MorestackSaveBase = 16 as MachineWord

// The saved GPRs, in slot order: the eight argument registers, the ten callee-saved, then x29 and x30.
let arm64MorestackSavedGprs = [Arm64Register.x0, Arm64Register.x1, Arm64Register.x2, Arm64Register.x3, Arm64Register.x4, Arm64Register.x5, Arm64Register.x6, Arm64Register.x7, Arm64Register.x19, Arm64Register.x20, Arm64Register.x21, Arm64Register.x22, Arm64Register.x23, Arm64Register.x24, Arm64Register.x25, Arm64Register.x26, Arm64Register.x27, Arm64Register.x28, Arm64Register.x29, Arm64Register.x30]

// The saved SIMD registers: the eight float ARGUMENT registers, and only those — see the ENTRY CONTRACT.
let arm64MorestackSavedFps = [Arm64Register.d0, Arm64Register.d1, Arm64Register.d2, Arm64Register.d3, Arm64Register.d4, Arm64Register.d5, Arm64Register.d6, Arm64Register.d7]

// The slot the LIVE x29 is saved in — written by this chunk with the REBASED value, so the restore hands the
// guarded function a frame pointer into the new stack. Derived from the register list's own position rather
// than written as a number, exactly as x64's `savedRbpDisp` is.
function arm64MorestackFrameSlot(index RegCount) returns MachineWord
	return Arm64MorestackSaveBase + (index as MachineWord) * SpillSlotBytes
end 'arm64MorestackFrameSlot'

function arm64MorestackFpSlot(index RegCount) returns MachineWord
	return arm64MorestackFrameSlot(arm64MorestackSavedGprs.count()) + (index as MachineWord) * SpillSlotBytes
end 'arm64MorestackFpSlot'

// The whole frame, 16-aligned. SP at the `bl mmap` is `systemStackTop - frameBytes`, and the system stack's
// top is page-aligned (`__sched_init_procs` takes it from `osAllocPages`), so a 16-aligned frame lands SP
// on AAPCS64's required residue. Asserted rather than assumed, because a misaligned SP on AArch64 is an
// alignment FAULT at the first frame access inside libSystem and names nothing that caused it.
function arm64MorestackFrameBytes() returns StackSize
	let raw = arm64MorestackFpSlot(arm64MorestackSavedFps.count()) as StackSize
	return alignUpStack(raw, alignment: StackFrameAlignment)
end 'arm64MorestackFrameBytes'

function buildArm64GtMorestackChunk(target Target) returns FunctionCodeChunk
	var asm = Arm64RuntimeAsm.beginRawFunction()
	let frameBytes = arm64MorestackFrameBytes()
	let doubling = asm.newLabel()
	let exhausted = asm.newLabel()
	let refused = asm.newLabel()

	// Step 1: ferry the GT stack pointer and switch to the system stack.
	arm64EmitMovFromSp(asm.code, dest: arm64RuntimeReg(Arm64MoreSpReg))
	arm64EmitMovToSp(asm.code, src: arm64RuntimeReg(Arm64MorestackEntrySysTopReg))
	arm64EmitStackAlloc(asm.code, amount: frameBytes)
	arm64GtStoreSp(asm, src: Arm64MoreSpReg, byteOffset: Arm64MorestackEntrySpSlot)

	// Step 2: save everything the ENTRY CONTRACT names, then take the GT into a body register.
	emitArm64MorestackRegisterBlock(asm, direction: Arm64GtBlockDirection.push)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeReg(asm, dest: Arm64MoreGtReg, src: Arm64MorestackEntryGtReg)

	// Step 3: old_base, old_size; TempB = need = old_top - projected + guard height; TempA = the ceiling;
	// new_size = old_size doubled until it holds `need`. The ceiling is tested ahead of the fit, so the loop ends
	// even on a `need` no stack could hold, and a refusal comes BEFORE anything is allocated.
	arm64EmitLoadWord64At(asm, dest: Arm64MoreOldBaseReg, base: Arm64MoreGtReg, byteOffset: GtOffStackBase)
	arm64EmitLoadWord64At(asm, dest: Arm64MoreOldSizeReg, base: Arm64MoreGtReg, byteOffset: GtOffStackSize)
	arm64EmitAddRuntimeReg(asm, dest: Arm64MoreTempB, lhs: Arm64MoreOldBaseReg, rhs: Arm64MoreOldSizeReg)
	arm64EmitSubRuntimeReg(asm, dest: Arm64MoreTempB, lhs: Arm64MoreTempB, rhs: Arm64MorestackEntryProjectedSpReg)
	arm64EmitAddRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64MoreTempB, base: Arm64MoreTempB, imm: gtStackGuardHeight(target) as Imm32)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64MoreTempA, value: GtMaxStackBytes as MachineWord)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeReg(asm, dest: Arm64MoreNewSizeReg, src: Arm64MoreOldSizeReg)

	asm.placeLabel(doubling)
	arm64EmitAddRuntimeReg(asm, dest: Arm64MoreNewSizeReg, lhs: Arm64MoreNewSizeReg, rhs: Arm64MoreNewSizeReg)
	arm64EmitCmpRuntimeReg(asm, lhs: Arm64MoreNewSizeReg, rhs: Arm64MoreTempA)
	arm64EmitCondBranchTo(asm, cond: Arm64CondCode.hi, label: exhausted)
	arm64EmitCmpRuntimeReg(asm, lhs: Arm64MoreNewSizeReg, rhs: Arm64MoreTempB)
	arm64EmitCondBranchTo(asm, cond: Arm64CondCode.lo, label: doubling)

	// Step 4: new_base = mmap(NULL, new_size, RW, ANON|PRIVATE, -1, 0). A refusal is `MAP_FAILED` (-1) from
	// libSystem and a negative errno from a raw `svc`, so one signed test catches both.
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x0, value: MmapKernelChoosesBase)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeReg(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x1, src: Arm64MoreNewSizeReg)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x2, value: MmapProtReadWrite)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x3, value: mmapAnonPrivateFlags(target.os))
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x4, value: MmapAnonFd)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x5, value: MmapNoOffset)
	arm64EmitOsPrimitive(asm, os: target.os, importSlot: OsImportSlot.mmap, syscallNumber: Arm64LinuxSyscallMmap)
	arm64EmitCmpRuntimeImm(asm, reg: Arm64IntReturnReg, imm: 0)
	arm64EmitCondBranchTo(asm, cond: Arm64CondCode.le, label: refused)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeReg(asm, dest: Arm64MoreNewBaseReg, src: Arm64IntReturnReg)

	// Step 5: present the thread where it stands — `gt.sp` = the ferried SP, `gt.fp` = the live x29, which this
	// chunk never writes — and move it. Writing the two words of a RUNNING thread is harmless: nothing reads them
	// until its next switch-out overwrites both.
	arm64GtLoadSp(asm, dest: Arm64MoreTempA, byteOffset: Arm64MorestackEntrySpSlot)
	arm64EmitStoreWord64At(asm, src: Arm64MoreTempA, base: Arm64MoreGtReg, byteOffset: GtOffSp)
	arm64EmitStoreWord64At(asm, src: Arm64Register.x29, base: Arm64MoreGtReg, byteOffset: GtOffFp)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeReg(asm, dest: Arm64RelocGtReg, src: Arm64MoreGtReg)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeReg(asm, dest: Arm64RelocNewBaseReg, src: Arm64MoreNewBaseReg)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeReg(asm, dest: Arm64RelocNewSizeReg, src: Arm64MoreNewSizeReg)
	arm64EmitBranchLink(asm.code, callFixups: asm.callFixups, targetName: GtStackRelocateName)

	// Step 6: both stack marks (`GtStackMarksOfAGrow`) — the size was chosen now, and the thread has grown — and
	// the rebased live x29 goes to its saved slot so the restore below hands it back.
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64MoreTempA, value: GtStackMarksOfAGrow as MachineWord)
	arm64EmitStoreWord64At(asm, src: Arm64MoreTempA, base: Arm64MoreGtReg, byteOffset: GtOffStackMarks)
	arm64EmitLoadWord64At(asm, dest: Arm64MoreTempA, base: Arm64MoreGtReg, byteOffset: GtOffFp)
	arm64GtStoreSp(asm, src: Arm64MoreTempA, byteOffset: arm64MorestackFrameSlot(arm64MorestackLiveFpIndex()))

	// Step 7: tear down and return onto the RELOCATED stack, at the rebased `gt.sp`, read BEFORE the register
	// restores because the GT's register is one of the registers those restores overwrite.
	arm64EmitLoadWord64At(asm, dest: Arm64MoreSpReg, base: Arm64MoreGtReg, byteOffset: GtOffSp)
	emitArm64MorestackRegisterBlock(asm, direction: Arm64GtBlockDirection.pop)
	arm64EmitStackFree(asm.code, amount: frameBytes)
	arm64EmitMovToSp(asm.code, src: arm64RuntimeReg(Arm64MoreSpReg))
	arm64EmitRet(asm.code)

	// The two refusals, on the system stack with SP 16-aligned by `arm64MorestackFrameBytes`.
	asm.placeLabel(exhausted)
	arm64EmitProcessExit(asm, os: target.os, exitCode: RuntimeAbort.greenThreadStackExhausted.rawValue as MachineWord)
	asm.placeLabel(refused)
	arm64EmitProcessExit(asm, os: target.os, exitCode: RuntimeAbort.greenThreadStackAllocationFailed.rawValue as MachineWord)

	return asm.finishChunk(GtMorestackName)
end 'buildArm64GtMorestackChunk'

// The index of x29 within `arm64MorestackSavedGprs` — found rather than written down, so moving the list
// cannot leave the live-frame-pointer rebase writing into some other register's slot.
function arm64MorestackLiveFpIndex() returns RegCount
	for k in 0 upto arm64MorestackSavedGprs.count() 'find'
		let reg = try arm64MorestackSavedGprs.get(k) otherwise panic("arm64MorestackLiveFpIndex: index derived from count is in range")

		if reg == Arm64Register.x29 'found'
			return k
		end 'found'
	end 'find'

	panic("arm64MorestackLiveFpIndex: `arm64MorestackSavedGprs` does not save x29 — the relocating grower must rebase the LIVE frame pointer in its own saved slot, or the guarded function resumes with a frame pointer into the stack that was just unmapped")
end 'arm64MorestackLiveFpIndex'

// This chunk's own SP-relative frame slots — SP-relative rather than x29-relative because morestack has no
// frame RECORD: it is the one runtime chunk whose frame lives on a stack it switched to itself. The
// encoders are `Arm64Backend`'s, which is where SP's base-field value is stated (see their header for the
// M1 trap that keeps it there).
function arm64GtStoreSp(asm Arm64RuntimeAsm, src Arm64Register, byteOffset MachineWord)
	arm64EmitStoreWord64Sp(asm.code, src: arm64RuntimeReg(src), byteOffset: byteOffset)
end 'arm64GtStoreSp'

function arm64GtLoadSp(asm Arm64RuntimeAsm, dest Arm64Register, byteOffset MachineWord)
	arm64EmitLoadWord64Sp(asm.code, dest: arm64RuntimeReg(dest), byteOffset: byteOffset)
end 'arm64GtLoadSp'

// Save or restore morestack's whole register block, from ONE list walk in both directions — the reason
// `emitArm64GtSavedBlock` gives, in a chunk where the consequence of a mismatch is a caller's argument
// silently replaced by a kernel return value.
function emitArm64MorestackRegisterBlock(asm Arm64RuntimeAsm, direction Arm64GtBlockDirection)
	for k in 0 upto arm64MorestackSavedGprs.count() 'gprs'
		let reg = try arm64MorestackSavedGprs.get(k) otherwise panic("emitArm64MorestackRegisterBlock: index derived from count is in range")
		let slot = arm64MorestackFrameSlot(k)

		match direction 'dir'
			push then arm64GtStoreSp(asm, src: reg, byteOffset: slot)
			pop then arm64GtLoadSp(asm, dest: reg, byteOffset: slot)
		end 'dir'
	end 'gprs'

	for k in 0 upto arm64MorestackSavedFps.count() 'fps'
		let reg = try arm64MorestackSavedFps.get(k) otherwise panic("emitArm64MorestackRegisterBlock: index derived from count is in range")
		let slot = arm64MorestackFpSlot(k)

		match direction 'dir'
			push then arm64EmitStoreFpSp(asm.code, src: arm64RuntimeFpReg(reg), byteOffset: slot)
			pop then arm64EmitLoadFpSp(asm.code, dest: arm64RuntimeFpReg(reg), byteOffset: slot)
		end 'dir'
	end 'fps'
end 'emitArm64MorestackRegisterBlock'

// The registers `__gt_stack_relocate` works in: its three AAPCS64 arguments, then caller-saved scratch. It makes
// no call until its last step, the release, after which none of them is read, so nothing is saved but its frame
// record.
let Arm64RelocGtReg = Arm64Register.x0
let Arm64RelocNewBaseReg = Arm64Register.x1
let Arm64RelocNewSizeReg = Arm64Register.x2
let Arm64RelocOldBaseReg = Arm64Register.x3
let Arm64RelocOldSizeReg = Arm64Register.x4
let Arm64RelocOldTopReg = Arm64Register.x5
let Arm64RelocOffsetReg = Arm64Register.x6  // new_top - old_top
let Arm64RelocNewLowReg = Arm64Register.x7  // the relocated `gt.sp`: the lowest live word on the new stack
let Arm64RelocNewHighReg = Arm64Register.x11  // new_top
let Arm64RelocWalkReg = Arm64Register.x12
let Arm64RelocLinkReg = Arm64Register.x13
let Arm64RelocSrcReg = Arm64Register.x14
let Arm64RelocDstReg = Arm64Register.x15

// ⭐⭐⭐ `__gt_stack_relocate(x0 = gt, x1 = newBase, x2 = newSize)` — see `GtRuntime.GtStackRelocateName`. Called
// from `__gt_morestack` on the system stack and from `__gt_stack_shrink` on a machine's scheduler context, so its
// release never runs on the stack it releases. It copies only `[gt.sp, old_top)`, which is what lets the same
// routine move a thread onto a SMALLER stack; the caller has established that it fits.
//
// It is FRAMED, unlike the grower, which keeps x30 in its register block: the release is a call, and the frame
// record is where this chunk's own return address survives it.
function buildArm64GtStackRelocateChunk(target Target) returns FunctionCodeChunk
	var asm = Arm64RuntimeAsm.beginFunction(NoRelocateSlots)
	let copyLoop = asm.newLabel()
	let copyDone = asm.newLabel()

	arm64EmitLoadWord64At(asm, dest: Arm64RelocOldBaseReg, base: Arm64RelocGtReg, byteOffset: GtOffStackBase)
	arm64EmitLoadWord64At(asm, dest: Arm64RelocOldSizeReg, base: Arm64RelocGtReg, byteOffset: GtOffStackSize)
	arm64EmitAddRuntimeReg(asm, dest: Arm64RelocOldTopReg, lhs: Arm64RelocOldBaseReg, rhs: Arm64RelocOldSizeReg)
	arm64EmitAddRuntimeReg(asm, dest: Arm64RelocNewHighReg, lhs: Arm64RelocNewBaseReg, rhs: Arm64RelocNewSizeReg)

	// offset = new_top - old_top: every address in the live region moves by that one amount, because the region
	// is copied to the TOP of the new stack.
	arm64EmitSubRuntimeReg(asm, dest: Arm64RelocOffsetReg, lhs: Arm64RelocNewHighReg, rhs: Arm64RelocOldTopReg)

	// Copy `[gt.sp, old_top)` word by word — `gt.sp` is 16-aligned and the top a page boundary, so the region is a
	// whole number of words. AArch64 has no string instruction.
	arm64EmitLoadWord64At(asm, dest: Arm64RelocSrcReg, base: Arm64RelocGtReg, byteOffset: GtOffSp)
	arm64EmitAddRuntimeReg(asm, dest: Arm64RelocNewLowReg, lhs: Arm64RelocSrcReg, rhs: Arm64RelocOffsetReg)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeReg(asm, dest: Arm64RelocDstReg, src: Arm64RelocNewLowReg)

	asm.placeLabel(copyLoop)
	arm64EmitCmpRuntimeReg(asm, lhs: Arm64RelocSrcReg, rhs: Arm64RelocOldTopReg)
	arm64EmitCondBranchTo(asm, cond: Arm64CondCode.hs, label: copyDone)
	arm64EmitLoadWord64At(asm, dest: Arm64RelocLinkReg, base: Arm64RelocSrcReg, byteOffset: 0)
	arm64EmitStoreWord64At(asm, src: Arm64RelocLinkReg, base: Arm64RelocDstReg, byteOffset: 0)
	arm64EmitAddRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64RelocSrcReg, base: Arm64RelocSrcReg, imm: SpillSlotBytes as Imm32)
	arm64EmitAddRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64RelocDstReg, base: Arm64RelocDstReg, imm: SpillSlotBytes as Imm32)
	arm64EmitBranchTo(asm, label: copyLoop)
	asm.placeLabel(copyDone)
	arm64EmitStoreWord64At(asm, src: Arm64RelocNewLowReg, base: Arm64RelocGtReg, byteOffset: GtOffSp)

	// `[gt.sp]`: a parked thread's x29 as its switch record holds it, or at a grow the guard's saved x30 — see this
	// section's header for why one range test serves both.
	emitArm64RelocateRebaseWord(asm, reg: Arm64RelocLinkReg, base: Arm64RelocNewLowReg, byteOffset: 0)

	// `gt.fp` rebased in place, then the chain from it.
	emitArm64RelocateRebaseWord(asm, reg: Arm64RelocWalkReg, base: Arm64RelocGtReg, byteOffset: GtOffFp)
	emitArm64RelocateChainWalk(asm)

	// The GT's own view of its stack. There is no TIB pair on this lane (this file's header, item 1).
	arm64EmitStoreWord64At(asm, src: Arm64RelocNewBaseReg, base: Arm64RelocGtReg, byteOffset: GtOffStackBase)
	arm64EmitStoreWord64At(asm, src: Arm64RelocNewSizeReg, base: Arm64RelocGtReg, byteOffset: GtOffStackSize)
	arm64EmitAddRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64RelocLinkReg, base: Arm64RelocNewBaseReg, imm: gtStackGuardHeight(target) as Imm32)
	arm64EmitStoreWord64At(asm, src: Arm64RelocLinkReg, base: Arm64RelocGtReg, byteOffset: GtOffStackGuard)

	// Give the old stack back. Every pointer into it has been rewritten, so nothing that survives this can read it.
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeReg(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x0, src: Arm64RelocOldBaseReg)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeReg(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x1, src: Arm64RelocOldSizeReg)
	arm64EmitOsPrimitive(asm, os: target.os, importSlot: OsImportSlot.munmap, syscallNumber: Arm64LinuxSyscallMunmap)

	return asm.finishFunction(GtStackRelocateName)
end 'buildArm64GtStackRelocateChunk'

// `__gt_stack_relocate` keeps nothing in its frame but the record.
let NoRelocateSlots = 0 as StackSize

// `reg += offset` IFF `reg` points inside the OLD stack `[old_base, old_top)`. The one statement of that test, for
// the switch record's word, `gt.fp` and each interior chain link — a second copy is where one of them would come
// to test a bound the others do not.
//
// ⚠ **UNSIGNED COMPARES.** These are addresses; a signed `lt` would read a stack near the top of the
// address space as negative and skip every rebase, which is a use-after-free rather than a crash.
function emitArm64RelocateRebase(asm Arm64RuntimeAsm, reg Arm64Register)
	let skip = asm.newLabel()

	arm64EmitCmpRuntimeReg(asm, lhs: reg, rhs: Arm64RelocOldBaseReg)
	arm64EmitCondBranchTo(asm, cond: Arm64CondCode.lo, label: skip)
	arm64EmitCmpRuntimeReg(asm, lhs: reg, rhs: Arm64RelocOldTopReg)
	arm64EmitCondBranchTo(asm, cond: Arm64CondCode.hs, label: skip)
	arm64EmitAddRuntimeReg(asm, dest: reg, lhs: reg, rhs: Arm64RelocOffsetReg)

	asm.placeLabel(skip)
end 'emitArm64RelocateRebase'

// The word at `[base + byteOffset]` rebased in place, left in `reg` for a caller that follows it.
function emitArm64RelocateRebaseWord(asm Arm64RuntimeAsm, reg Arm64Register, base Arm64Register, byteOffset MachineWord)
	arm64EmitLoadWord64At(asm, dest: reg, base: base, byteOffset: byteOffset)
	emitArm64RelocateRebase(asm, reg: reg)
	arm64EmitStoreWord64At(asm, src: reg, base: base, byteOffset: byteOffset)
end 'emitArm64RelocateRebaseWord'

// The saved-x29 chain walk — the correctness crux. Entered with the walker (the rebased `gt.fp`) in
// `Arm64RelocWalkReg`. For each frame record whose link points into the OLD stack it adds the relocation offset,
// then follows the now-adjusted link. It ends when the walker leaves the relocated region `[new_low, new_high)`,
// which a null link does too: a green thread's outermost frame is the trampoline's, whose x29 is the seed zero.
function emitArm64RelocateChainWalk(asm Arm64RuntimeAsm)
	let head = asm.newLabel()
	let done = asm.newLabel()

	asm.placeLabel(head)
	arm64EmitCmpRuntimeReg(asm, lhs: Arm64RelocWalkReg, rhs: Arm64RelocNewLowReg)
	arm64EmitCondBranchTo(asm, cond: Arm64CondCode.lo, label: done)
	arm64EmitCmpRuntimeReg(asm, lhs: Arm64RelocWalkReg, rhs: Arm64RelocNewHighReg)
	arm64EmitCondBranchTo(asm, cond: Arm64CondCode.hs, label: done)

	// [walker] is the caller's saved x29 — the frame record's first word (`[x29+8]` is the return address, which
	// points into `.text` and does not move).
	emitArm64RelocateRebaseWord(asm, reg: Arm64RelocLinkReg, base: Arm64RelocWalkReg, byteOffset: 0)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeReg(asm, dest: Arm64RelocWalkReg, src: Arm64RelocLinkReg)
	arm64EmitBranchTo(asm, label: head)

	asm.placeLabel(done)
end 'emitArm64RelocateChainWalk'

// ==========================================================================
// The prologue stack GUARD
// ==========================================================================

// The bytes the guard's COLD path opens on the green thread's stack to hold x30 across its
// `bl __gt_morestack`. Sixteen rather than eight, because AArch64 requires SP to stay 16-aligned at every
// access that uses it as a base — the ISA fact x64's guard does not have to buy, since its `call` pushes
// the return address itself.
//
// ⚠ It is added to the PROJECTION as well as opened on the cold path, so the guard fires while there is
// still room for the cell it is about to open. A projection that ignored it would let a green thread whose
// remaining stack is exactly the frame walk one cell past its own guard word.
let Arm64GuardSaveBytes = 16 as StackSize

// The registers the guard works in: caller-saved, non-argument, and outside every value live at a function
// ENTRY (only x0..x7 and d0..d7 are). `__gt_morestack`'s entry contract names the first two, which is why
// they are declared together with it rather than chosen here.
let Arm64GuardScratchReg = Arm64Register.x13

// ⭐ Emit the guard at the very START of a guarded function's entry block — BEFORE the prologue — for a
// USER function of a green-thread module that reserves a frame. When the running green thread's projected
// SP would dip below its stack guard, it grows and relocates the stack first; otherwise it is a handful of
// instructions that fall through. A no-op for a P's inline scheduler GT (`stackGuard` is the slab zero, so
// the `hs` is always taken) and off a green thread entirely (the three null tests bail). `mrt_panic` runs it
// too, after its frame record (`Arm64PanicRuntime.arm64EmitPanicStackGuard`).
//
// ⚠ **IT PRESERVES x30 ITSELF**, which is the whole shape difference from x64's — see this file's header.
//
// It emits into the function's own byte stream with a GUARD-LOCAL branch table, exactly as the x64 twin
// does, so it is invisible to the Target-IR goldens and is verified by the RUN plus a disassembly instead.
export function emitArm64GtStackGuard(code ByteArray, callFixups CallFixupArray, rdataRelocs RdataRelocationArray, frameBytes StackSize, margin ParsedInt, os Os)
	var asm = Arm64RuntimeAsm.beginRawFunction()
	let skip = asm.newLabel()

	emitArm64LoadCurrentGtOrBail(asm, mDest: Arm64MorestackEntryGtReg, gtDest: Arm64MorestackEntryGtReg, scratch: Arm64GuardScratchReg, bail: skip, os: os)

	// projected = SP - (frameBytes + margin + the cold path's own cell); enough room (>= gt.stackGuard) → skip.
	arm64EmitMovFromSp(asm.code, dest: arm64RuntimeReg(Arm64MorestackEntrySysTopReg))
	arm64EmitAddSubImmWide(asm.code, opcode: Arm64OpSubImm, rd: arm64RuntimeReg(Arm64MorestackEntrySysTopReg), rn: arm64RuntimeReg(Arm64MorestackEntrySysTopReg), imm: ((frameBytes + (margin as StackSize) + Arm64GuardSaveBytes) as MachineWord))
	arm64EmitLoadWord64At(asm, dest: Arm64GuardScratchReg, base: Arm64MorestackEntryGtReg, byteOffset: GtOffStackGuard)
	arm64EmitCmpRuntimeReg(asm, lhs: Arm64MorestackEntrySysTopReg, rhs: Arm64GuardScratchReg)
	arm64EmitCondBranchTo(asm, cond: Arm64CondCode.hs, label: skip)

	// Cold path. x30 is the guarded function's return address and the prologue has not saved it yet, so this
	// is the one place that must. The cell rides the GT stack, which `__gt_morestack` copies and relocates —
	// so the reload below reads the copy, at the SP the grower handed back.
	arm64EmitStackAlloc(asm.code, amount: Arm64GuardSaveBytes)
	arm64GtStoreSp(asm, src: Arm64Register.x30, byteOffset: 0)

	// x14 = the projected SP and x12 = this M's system-stack top, which IS `__gt_morestack`'s entry contract.
	// The M is re-read rather than kept from the test above, because that register now holds the current GT —
	// the OTHER part of the contract — and the hot path must not spend a register to save a load on the cold one.
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeReg(asm, dest: Arm64MorestackEntryProjectedSpReg, src: Arm64MorestackEntrySysTopReg)
	emitArm64LoadCurrentMOrBailless(asm, dest: Arm64MorestackEntrySysTopReg, scratch: Arm64GuardScratchReg, os: os)
	arm64EmitLoadWord64At(asm, dest: Arm64MorestackEntrySysTopReg, base: Arm64MorestackEntrySysTopReg, byteOffset: MOffSystemStackSP)
	arm64EmitBranchLink(asm.code, callFixups: asm.callFixups, targetName: GtMorestackName)
	arm64GtLoadSp(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x30, byteOffset: 0)
	arm64EmitStackFree(asm.code, amount: Arm64GuardSaveBytes)

	asm.placeLabel(skip)
	arm64AppendResolvedFragment(asm, code: code, callFixups: callFixups, rdataRelocs: rdataRelocs)
end 'emitArm64GtStackGuard'

// ⭐ The M alone, where the caller has already PROVEN it exists and therefore has no branch to take. Not a
// weaker version of `emitArm64LoadCurrentMOrBail` but a different PRECONDITION, and its one caller states
// the proof: the guard's cold path runs only after the guarded read above answered non-null, and a thread
// becomes an M once and never unbecomes one. A test here would need a branch target with no correct
// destination.
function emitArm64LoadCurrentMOrBailless(asm Arm64RuntimeAsm, dest Arm64Register, scratch Arm64Register, os Os)
	arm64EmitAdrpAddPair(asm.code, rdataRelocs: asm.rdataRelocs, dest: arm64RuntimeReg(scratch), label: SchedTlsTebOffsetLabel, adrpKind: RdataRelocKind.dataSectionArm64Adrp, pageOffKind: RdataRelocKind.dataSectionArm64PageOff)
	arm64EmitLoadWord64At(asm, dest: scratch, base: scratch, byteOffset: 0)
	arm64EmitThreadPointer(asm.code, dest: arm64RuntimeReg(dest), source: arm64ThreadPointerSource(os))
	arm64EmitLoadRegOffset(asm.code, dest: arm64RuntimeReg(dest), base: arm64RuntimeReg(dest), index: arm64RuntimeReg(scratch))
end 'emitArm64LoadCurrentMOrBailless'

// ==========================================================================
// The system-stack door — where every libSystem call a green thread makes runs
// ==========================================================================

// ⭐⭐⭐ **`mrt_darwin_libc_call` — RUN A libSystem CALL ON THE M's SYSTEM STACK WHEN IT WOULD OTHERWISE RUN ON
// A GREEN THREAD's OWN.** Go's `libcCall` → `asmcgocall`, which moves every Darwin libc call onto g0's stack. A
// green thread's stack is a small seed the relocating grower doubles on demand, and the grower is driven by
// the PROLOGUE GUARD, which sees only Maxon frames; a libSystem frame is invisible to it and unbounded —
// `realpath` alone outgrows a seed. The caller loads the import's address into x16
// (`arm64EmitLoadImportAddress`) and `bl`s here with the call's own arguments in place, and gets back the
// callee's answer with every callee-saved register intact.
//
// ⭐⭐ **ONE DOOR FOR COMPILED FUNCTIONS AND HAND-ASSEMBLED CHUNKS ALIKE** (`arm64EmitLibcCall`). A chunk is
// entered by `bl`, not through an `arm64ImportCall`, so a switch emitted only around that op would leave every
// `mrt_host_*` body calling libSystem on whatever stack it was entered on.
//
// ⛔⛔ **IT SWITCHES ONLY WHEN SP IS INSIDE THE CURRENT GREEN THREAD's OWN STACK.** The switch sets SP to the
// system stack's TOP, which is sound only when nothing is live there — true of a green thread's call, and
// false for two callers that run with a green thread current and SP elsewhere: `__gt_morestack`, whose own
// frame is on the system stack, and the fault handler, on the signal stack. Both take the direct road, as do
// an M's scheduler context (`stackBase` 0, a real OS-thread stack) and a thread that is no M at all.
//
// ⚠ **NO NESTING.** A libSystem call is a leaf that never re-enters Maxon code, and an OS thread runs one
// thing at a time, so no second door call can reach the same system stack while this one is on it.
//
// ⚠ **THE CALLER's OUTGOING-ARGUMENT REGION CROSSES WITH IT.** A variadic argument travels on the stack, in the
// region `Arm64RuntimeAsm.OneOutgoingArgWord` sizes (`beginVariadicCallerFunction`), so that region is copied
// to the system stack before the call; for every other caller the copy is words the callee never reads.
//
// ⚠ **THE SWITCHED ROAD LAYS A FRAME RECORD** holding the caller's return address, so a backtrace taken inside
// the callee walks through the door into its caller rather than skipping it.
//
// A program with no green threads has no system stack and no current-thread slot, so its door is the direct
// road alone.
let DarwinLibcCallName = b"mrt_darwin_libc_call"

export function buildArm64DarwinLibcCallChunk(greenThreads bool) returns FunctionCodeChunk
	var asm = Arm64RuntimeAsm.beginRawFunction()
	let direct = asm.newLabel()

	if greenThreads 'mayRunOnAGreenThread'
		emitArm64LoadCurrentGtOrBail(asm, mDest: Arm64DoorMReg, gtDest: Arm64DoorGtReg, scratch: Arm64DoorSpReg, bail: direct, os: Os.macos)
		arm64EmitMovFromSp(asm.code, dest: arm64RuntimeReg(Arm64DoorSpReg))
		emitArm64GtBailUnlessOnItsOwnStack(asm, gt: Arm64DoorGtReg, scratch: Arm64DoorLowReg, addr: Arm64DoorSpReg, bail: direct)

		// The green thread's SP goes in the system stack's top pair — twice, because a pair is what keeps SP
		// 16-aligned — and the frame record under it.
		arm64EmitLoadWord64At(asm, dest: Arm64DoorMReg, base: Arm64DoorMReg, byteOffset: MOffSystemStackSP)
		arm64EmitMovToSp(asm.code, src: arm64RuntimeReg(Arm64DoorMReg))
		arm64EmitPushPairGpr(asm.code, rt1: arm64RuntimeReg(Arm64DoorSpReg), rt2: arm64RuntimeReg(Arm64DoorSpReg))
		arm64EmitPushPairGpr(asm.code, rt1: arm64RuntimeReg(Arm64Register.x29), rt2: arm64RuntimeReg(Arm64Register.x30))
		arm64EmitMovFromSp(asm.code, dest: arm64RuntimeReg(Arm64Register.x29))

		// Pushed from the region's top pair down, so its lowest word lands at the new SP.
		let outgoingPairs = OneOutgoingArgWord / Arm64GtPairBytes

		for pair in 0 upto outgoingPairs 'copyOutgoingArgs'
			let pairOffset = (outgoingPairs - 1 - pair) * Arm64GtPairBytes
			arm64EmitLoadWord64At(asm, dest: Arm64DoorLowReg, base: Arm64DoorSpReg, byteOffset: pairOffset)
			arm64EmitLoadWord64At(asm, dest: Arm64DoorHighReg, base: Arm64DoorSpReg, byteOffset: pairOffset + SpillSlotBytes)
			arm64EmitPushPairGpr(asm.code, rt1: arm64RuntimeReg(Arm64DoorLowReg), rt2: arm64RuntimeReg(Arm64DoorHighReg))
		end 'copyOutgoingArgs'

		arm64EmitBranchLinkReg(asm.code, reg: Arm64ImportAddressReg)

		arm64EmitMovToSp(asm.code, src: arm64RuntimeReg(Arm64Register.x29))
		arm64EmitPopPairGpr(asm.code, rt1: arm64RuntimeReg(Arm64Register.x29), rt2: arm64RuntimeReg(Arm64Register.x30))
		arm64EmitPopPairGpr(asm.code, rt1: arm64RuntimeReg(Arm64DoorSpReg), rt2: arm64RuntimeReg(Arm64DoorLowReg))
		arm64EmitMovToSp(asm.code, src: arm64RuntimeReg(Arm64DoorSpReg))
		arm64EmitRet(asm.code)
	end 'mayRunOnAGreenThread'

	// The direct road is a tail call: the callee returns straight to the caller through the untouched x30.
	asm.placeLabel(direct)
	arm64EmitBranchReg(asm.code, reg: Arm64ImportAddressReg)

	return asm.finishChunk(DarwinLibcCallName)
end 'buildArm64DarwinLibcCallChunk'

// The door's scratch — caller-saved, non-argument, and neither x16 (the callee's address) nor x9 (the
// throwing-call error flag, which a call site close to a `tryCall` must not find borrowed). A call through the
// door destroys them as any call destroys the caller-saved file (`TargetDialect.arm64ImportCall`'s
// `implicitDefs`). The low/high pair is also the shuttle for the copied stack words.
let Arm64DoorMReg = Arm64Register.x10
let Arm64DoorGtReg = Arm64Register.x11
let Arm64DoorSpReg = Arm64Register.x12
let Arm64DoorLowReg = Arm64Register.x13
let Arm64DoorHighReg = Arm64Register.x14

// A libSystem call through the system-stack door: the import's address into x16, then `bl` the door. Every
// libSystem call on arm64-macOS takes it, except a green-thread-free program's compiled calls and the entry
// stub's `_exit`, which run only on OS-thread stacks.
export function arm64EmitLibcCall(code ByteArray, iatRelocs RelocationArray, callFixups CallFixupArray, slotIndex RelocSlotIndex)
	arm64EmitLoadImportAddress(code, iatRelocs: iatRelocs, slotIndex: slotIndex)
	arm64EmitBranchLink(code, callFixups: callFixups, targetName: DarwinLibcCallName)
end 'arm64EmitLibcCall'

// A compiled function's `arm64ImportCall`: through the door in a green-thread program, and direct in one with
// no green threads, whose every stack is an OS thread's.
export function emitArm64GtGatedImportCall(code ByteArray, callFixups CallFixupArray, iatRelocs RelocationArray, slotIndex RelocSlotIndex, greenThreads bool)
	if greenThreads 'throughTheDoor'
		arm64EmitLibcCall(code, iatRelocs: iatRelocs, callFixups: callFixups, slotIndex: slotIndex)
	end 'throughTheDoor' else 'direct'
		arm64EmitImportCall(code, iatRelocs: iatRelocs, slotIndex: slotIndex)
	end 'direct'
end 'emitArm64GtGatedImportCall'

// ⭐ Splice a finished `Arm64RuntimeAsm` FRAGMENT into an enclosing function's byte stream: resolve its own
// branches against its own label map FIRST (so its ids never collide with the enclosing function's block
// ids), then append its bytes and REBASE every fixup it filed by the offset it landed at.
//
// It is the arm64 twin of what x64's shim does inline with `resolveChunkRel32Fixups`. Its one fragment is the
// prologue stack guard's, which files calls and `.data` references and no import.
function arm64AppendResolvedFragment(asm Arm64RuntimeAsm, code ByteArray, callFixups CallFixupArray, rdataRelocs RdataRelocationArray)
	arm64ResolveBlockBranches(asm.code, blockOffsets: asm.labelOffsets, fixups: asm.branchFixups)

	let base = code.count()

	for emitted in asm.code 'bytes'
		code.push(emitted)
	end 'bytes'

	for fixup in asm.callFixups 'calls'
		callFixups.push(CallFixup.create(base + fixup.codeOffset, targetName: fixup.targetName))
	end 'calls'

	for reloc in asm.rdataRelocs 'rdata'
		var rebased = RdataRelocation.create(base + reloc.codeOffset, rdataLabel: reloc.rdataLabel, kind: reloc.kind)

		// `create` zeroes `rdataOffset` — carried across explicitly rather than left at that default, because
		// a fragment that ever files a WITHIN-object displacement would otherwise have it silently dropped.
		rebased.rdataOffset = reloc.rdataOffset
		rdataRelocs.push(rebased)
	end 'rdata'
end 'arm64AppendResolvedFragment'

// ==========================================================================
// Assembly
// ==========================================================================

// Append the hand-assembled green-thread chunks — called by `buildArm64Backend` when the module
// carries the scheduler, on the same `usesGt` bit `installGtRuntime` was gated on rather than a probe of
// the module. They lay out above user code like every other runtime chunk, so `mrt_start` keeps `.text`
// offset 0.
export function appendArm64GtRuntimeChunks(chunks FunctionCodeChunkArray, target Target)
	chunks.push(buildArm64GtContextSwitchChunk(target))
	chunks.push(buildArm64GtTrampolineChunk())
	chunks.push(buildArm64GtMorestackChunk(target))
	chunks.push(buildArm64GtStackRelocateChunk(target))
end 'appendArm64GtRuntimeChunks'
